# WiFi receive budget, lean profile.
#
# Overlay applied on top of sdkconfig.defaults and the conservative TLS overlay; neither is edited
# here. It lowers how much of the internal heap the WiFi driver can pin at once with received
# frames. The boards have no PSRAM, so every driver buffer lives in the same heap the TLS
# handshake and the request bodies need at the same moment.
#
# RX_BA_WIN 16 -> 6. The block-ack window is the number of aggregated (AMPDU) frames the driver
# may hold in its reorder buffer while it waits for a gap in the sequence to be filled; each
# frame held there is a received-data buffer of up to ~1.6 KB that stays allocated until the
# window moves on. 16 is the Kconfig default for boards whose WiFi buffers can spill into PSRAM;
# for a board without it Kconfig picks 6, and only the explicit 16 that sdkconfig.defaults
# inherits from the factory profile keeps it at 16 (esp_wifi/Kconfig, range 2..32). A window of
# 6 keeps AMPDU reception, and with it what is negotiated with the access point, as it is, and
# lowers the ceiling of what one lost frame can pin. The two compile-time checks in
# esp_wifi/src/wifi_init.c stay satisfied: 6 <= DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM (32) and
# 6 <= 2 * STATIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM (8).
#
# CSI_ENABLED y -> n. Channel state information is captured per received frame into buffers of
# about one KB per static RX buffer (Kconfig help), allocated for the lifetime of the driver.
# Nothing in this firmware or in its libraries calls esp_wifi_set_csi(); the memory is spent on
# a feature that is never read.
#
# STATIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM and DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM are deliberately not changed here. The Arduino
# core (WiFiGeneric.cpp, wifiLowLevelInit) overwrites both in the wifi_init_config_t it hands to
# esp_wifi_init() - static 4, dynamic 32, and the TX side as dynamic with a cache of 4 - unless
# the sketch calls WiFi.useStaticBuffers(true), which this firmware does not. The precompiled
# driver (libpp.a) reads its buffer counts from that structure and from nowhere else, so a value
# set here would land in sdkconfig.h and never reach the radio. The Kconfig entries keep their
# factory values so that the checks above are evaluated against what is committed.
CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_RX_BA_WIN=6
CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_CSI_ENABLED=n
